@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1246 = private unnamed_addr constant [488 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks that async functions do not call `time.sleep`.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABlocking an async function via a `time.sleep` call will block the entire\0Aevent loop, preventing it from executing other tasks while waiting for the\0A`time.sleep`, negating the benefits of asynchronous programming.\0A\0AInstead of `time.sleep`, use `asyncio.sleep`.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aasync def fetch():\0A    time.sleep(1)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aasync def fetch():\0A    await asyncio.sleep(1)\0A```\0A", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1247 = private unnamed_addr constant [44 x i8] c"Async functions should not call `time.sleep`", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1248 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1247, [8 x i8] c",\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1249 = private unnamed_addr constant [12 x i8] c"exec-builtin", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1250 = private unnamed_addr constant [408 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of the builtin `exec` function.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `exec()` function is insecure as it allows for arbitrary code\0Aexecution.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aexec(\22print('Hello World')\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `exec`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/functions.html#exec)\0A- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-78](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/78.html)\0A", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1251 = private unnamed_addr constant [22 x i8] c"Use of `exec` detected", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1252 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1251, [8 x i8] c"\16\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1253 = private unnamed_addr constant [22 x i8] c"snmp-weak-cryptography", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1254 = private unnamed_addr constant [525 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of the SNMPv3 protocol without encryption.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AUnencrypted SNMPv3 communication can be intercepted and read by\0Aunauthorized parties. Instead, enable encryption when using SNMPv3.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Afrom pysnmp.hlapi import UsmUserData\0A\0AUsmUserData(\22user\22)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Afrom pysnmp.hlapi import UsmUserData\0A\0AUsmUserData(\22user\22, \22authkey\22, \22privkey\22)\0A```\0A\0A## References\0A- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-319](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/319.html)\0A", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1255 = private unnamed_addr constant [88 x i8] c"You should not use SNMPv3 without encryption. `noAuthNoPriv` & `authNoPriv` is insecure.", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1256 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1255, [8 x i8] c"X\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1257 = private unnamed_addr constant [15 x i8] c"try-except-pass", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1258 = private unnamed_addr constant [768 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for uses of the `try`-`except`-`pass` pattern.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AThe `try`-`except`-`pass` pattern suppresses all exceptions. Suppressing\0Aexceptions may hide errors that could otherwise reveal unexpected behavior,\0Asecurity vulnerabilities, or malicious activity. Instead, consider logging\0Athe exception.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Atry:\0A    ...\0Aexcept Exception:\0A    pass\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport logging\0A\0Atry:\0A    ...\0Aexcept Exception as exc:\0A    logging.exception(\22Exception occurred\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Options\0A- `lint.flake8-bandit.check-typed-exception`\0A\0A## References\0A- [Common Weakness Enumeration: CWE-703](https://cwe.mitre.org/data/definitions/703.html)\0A- [Python documentation: `logging`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/logging.html)\0A", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1259 = private unnamed_addr constant [62 x i8] c"`try`-`except`-`pass` detected, consider logging the exception", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1260 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1259, [8 x i8] c">\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1261 = private unnamed_addr constant [31 x i8] c"batched-without-explicit-strict", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1262 = private unnamed_addr constant [1073 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `itertools.batched` calls without an explicit `strict` parameter.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ABy default, if the length of the iterable is not divisible by\0Athe second argument to `itertools.batched`, the last batch\0Awill be shorter than the rest.\0A\0AIn Python 3.13, a `strict` parameter was added which allows controlling if the batches must be of uniform length.\0APass `strict=True` to raise a `ValueError` if the batches are of non-uniform length.\0AOtherwise, pass `strict=False` to make the intention explicit.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aitertools.batched(iterable, n)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead if the batches must be of uniform length:\0A```python\0Aitertools.batched(iterable, n, strict=True)\0A```\0A\0AOr if the batches can be of non-uniform length:\0A```python\0Aitertools.batched(iterable, n, strict=False)\0A```\0A\0A## Known deviations\0AUnlike the upstream `B911`, this rule will not report infinite iterators\0A(e.g., `itertools.cycle(...)`).\0A\0A## Options\0A- `target-version`\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python documentation: `batched`](https://docs.python.org/3/library/itertools.html#batched)\0A", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1263 = private unnamed_addr constant [60 x i8] c"`itertools.batched()` without an explicit `strict` parameter", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1264 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1263, [8 x i8] c"<\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1265 = private unnamed_addr constant [33 x i8] c"unnecessary-comprehension-in-call", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1266 = private unnamed_addr constant [2241 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for unnecessary list or set comprehensions passed to builtin functions that take an iterable.\0A\0ASet comprehensions are only a violation in the case where the builtin function does not care about\0Aduplication of elements in the passed iterable.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0AMany builtin functions (this rule currently covers `any` and `all` in stable, along with `min`,\0A`max`, and `sum` in [preview]) accept any iterable, including a generator. Constructing a\0Atemporary list via list comprehension is unnecessary and wastes memory for large iterables.\0A\0A`any` and `all` can also short-circuit iteration, saving a lot of time. The unnecessary\0Acomprehension forces a full iteration of the input iterable, giving up the benefits of\0Ashort-circuiting. For example, compare the performance of `all` with a list comprehension\0Aagainst that of a generator in a case where an early short-circuit is possible (almost 40x\0Afaster):\0A\0A```console\0AIn [1]: %timeit all([i for i in range(1000)])\0A8.14 \C2\B5s \C2\B1 25.4 ns per loop (mean \C2\B1 std. dev. of 7 runs, 100,000 loops each)\0A\0AIn [2]: %timeit all(i for i in range(1000))\0A212 ns \C2\B1 0.892 ns per loop (mean \C2\B1 std. dev. of 7 runs, 1,000,000 loops each)\0A```\0A\0AThis performance improvement is due to short-circuiting. If the entire iterable has to be\0Atraversed, the comprehension version may even be a bit faster: list allocation overhead is not\0Anecessarily greater than generator overhead.\0A\0AApplying this rule simplifies the code and will usually save memory, but in the absence of\0Ashort-circuiting it may not improve performance. (It may even slightly regress performance,\0Athough the difference will usually be small.)\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Aany([x.id for x in bar])\0Aall([x.id for x in bar])\0Asum([x.val for x in bar])\0Amin([x.val for x in bar])\0Amax([x.val for x in bar])\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aany(x.id for x in bar)\0Aall(x.id for x in bar)\0Asum(x.val for x in bar)\0Amin(x.val for x in bar)\0Amax(x.val for x in bar)\0A```\0A\0A## Fix safety\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe, as it can change the behavior of the code if the iteration\0Ahas side effects (due to laziness and short-circuiting). The fix may also drop comments when\0Arewriting some comprehensions.\0A\0A[preview]: https://docs.astral.sh/ruff/preview/\0A", align 1
