@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1284 = private unnamed_addr constant [751 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for packages that are missing an `__init__.py` file.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0APython packages are directories that contain a file named `__init__.py`.\0AThe existence of this file indicates that the directory is a Python\0Apackage, and so it can be imported the same way a module can be\0Aimported.\0A\0ADirectories that lack an `__init__.py` file can still be imported, but\0Athey're indicative of a special kind of package, known as a \22namespace\0Apackage\22 (see: [PEP 420](https://peps.python.org/pep-0420/)).\0ANamespace packages are less widely used, so a package that lacks an\0A`__init__.py` file is typically meant to be a regular package, and\0Athe absence of the `__init__.py` file is probably an oversight.\0A\0A## Options\0A- `namespace-packages`\0A", align 1
