@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1372 = private unnamed_addr constant [1328 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for function docstrings that state that exceptions could be raised\0Aeven though they are not directly raised in the function body.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0ASome conventions prefer non-explicit exceptions be omitted from the\0Adocstring.\0A\0AThis rule is not enforced for abstract methods. It is also ignored for\0A\22stub functions\22: functions where the body only consists of `pass`, `...`,\0A`raise NotImplementedError`, or similar.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef calculate_speed(distance: float, time: float) -> float:\0A    \22\22\22Calculate speed as distance divided by time.\0A\0A    Args:\0A        distance: Distance traveled.\0A        time: Time spent traveling.\0A\0A    Returns:\0A        Speed as distance divided by time.\0A\0A    Raises:\0A        ZeroDivisionError: Divided by zero.\0A    \22\22\22\0A    return distance / time\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Adef calculate_speed(distance: float, time: float) -> float:\0A    \22\22\22Calculate speed as distance divided by time.\0A\0A    Args:\0A        distance: Distance traveled.\0A        time: Time spent traveling.\0A\0A    Returns:\0A        Speed as distance divided by time.\0A    \22\22\22\0A    return distance / time\0A```\0A\0A## Known issues\0AIt may often be desirable to document *all* exceptions that a function\0Acould possibly raise, even those which are not explicitly raised using\0A`raise` statements in the function body.\0A", align 1
