@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1509 = private unnamed_addr constant [1150 x i8] c"## What it does\0AChecks for `import` statements outside of a module's top-level scope, such\0Aas within a function or class definition.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A[PEP 8] recommends placing imports not only at the top-level of a module,\0Abut at the very top of the file, \22just after any module comments and\0Adocstrings, and before module globals and constants.\22\0A\0A`import` statements have effects that are global in scope; defining them at\0Athe top level has a number of benefits. For example, it makes it easier to\0Aidentify the dependencies of a module, and ensures that any invalid imports\0Aare caught regardless of whether a specific function is called or class is\0Ainstantiated.\0A\0AAn import statement would typically be placed within a function only to\0Aavoid a circular dependency, to defer a costly module load, or to avoid\0Aloading a dependency altogether in a certain runtime environment.\0A\0A## Example\0A```python\0Adef print_python_version():\0A    import platform\0A\0A    print(python.python_version())\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A```python\0Aimport platform\0A\0A\0Adef print_python_version():\0A    print(python.python_version())\0A```\0A\0A[PEP 8]: https://peps.python.org/pep-0008/#imports\0A", align 1
