@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1606 = private unnamed_addr constant [1055 x i8] c"## What it does\0A\0AChecks for uses of the `re` module that can be replaced with builtin `str` methods.\0A\0A## Why is this bad?\0A\0APerforming checks on strings directly can make the code simpler, may require\0Aless escaping, and will often be faster.\0A\0A## Example\0A\0A```python\0Are.sub(\22abc\22, \22\22, s)\0A```\0A\0AUse instead:\0A\0A```python\0As.replace(\22abc\22, \22\22)\0A```\0A\0A## Details\0A\0AThe rule reports the following calls when the first argument to the call is\0Aa plain string literal, and no additional flags are passed:\0A\0A- `re.sub`\0A- `re.match`\0A- `re.search`\0A- `re.fullmatch`\0A- `re.split`\0A\0AFor `re.sub`, the `repl` (replacement) argument must also be a string literal,\0Anot a function. For `re.match`, `re.search`, and `re.fullmatch`, the return\0Avalue must also be used only for its truth value.\0A\0A## Fix safety\0A\0AThis rule's fix is marked as unsafe if the affected expression contains comments. Otherwise,\0Athe fix can be applied safely.\0A\0A## References\0A- [Python Regular Expression HOWTO: Common Problems - Use String Methods](https://docs.python.org/3/howto/regex.html#use-string-methods)\0A", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1607 = private unnamed_addr constant [44 x i8] c"Plain string pattern passed to `re` function", align 1
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1608 = private unnamed_addr constant <{ ptr, [8 x i8] }> <{ ptr @anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1607, [8 x i8] c",\00\00\00\00\00\00\00" }>, align 8
@anon.8cc3e55251025b29006123e4bed3261b.1609 = private unnamed_addr constant [16 x i8] c"unraw-re-pattern", align 1
